<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georg Philipp Telemann</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1681–<a href="/years/1767">1767</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Georg Philipp Telemann was a German composer and one of the most prolific of all time. After posts at Sorau and as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapellmeister">Kapellmeister</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a> court from 1708, he worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> and from 1721 until his death as director of music for the five main churches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>. He wrote operas, cantatas, passions and much instrumental music, and was godfather to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-philipp-emanuel-bach">Carl Philipp Emanuel Bach</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georg-philipp-telemann.jpg" alt="Georg Philipp Telemann" width="320" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Valentin Daniel Preisler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Telemann.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Georg Philipp Telemann was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> and was almost completely self-taught in music. His father, a deacon, died when he was four. He had his first lessons at ten, and although his mother and relatives forbade musical activity, he studied and composed in secret, even writing an opera at the age of 12. At the Gymnasium Andreanum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a> he taught himself flute, oboe, violin, viola da gamba, recorder and double bass. In 1701 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> to study law but became a professional musician, writing for the Nikolaikirche and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-thomas-church-leipzig">Thomaskirche</a>. In 1702 he became director of the municipal opera house, and he supplied a wealth of music for Leipzig, including his first major opera, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Telemann left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in 1705 to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapellmeister">Kapellmeister</a> at the court of Count Erdmann II of Promnitz at Sorau, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> cut his time there short. He then entered the service of Duke Johann Wilhelm at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> had been born, and rose from Konzertmeister to Kapellmeister. In 1712 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> as city music director, where he gained his mature personal style and published his first works, including the Frankfurt Sonatas of 1715 and the Brockes Passion of 1716. In 1721 he took the post in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> of Kantor of the Johanneum Lateinschule and Kapellmeister of the five largest churches.</p><p>The next year, when Johann Kuhnau died, Telemann was approved as his successor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> but declined after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> gave him a raise, and the post went to Bach. He travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1737 and stayed eight months, and after hearing Rameau's Castor et Pollux he added French operatic style to his vocal works. He was married twice. His first wife died soon after the birth of their daughter, and his second wife, Maria Catharina Textor, bore nine children but later accumulated large gambling debts. He died on 25 June 1767, aged 86.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Telemann composed more than 3,000 works, half of them lost, including 1,043 sacred cantatas and 600 overture-suites between 1708 and 1750. He combined German, French, Italian and Polish styles in what is called the German mixed style. His contemporaries rated him highly, and Bach and Handel bought and studied his published works. By pursuing exclusive publication rights, he set an early precedent for treating music as the intellectual property of the composer. In the early 19th century his reputation fell, and critics called him a Vielschreiber, someone for whom quantity came before quality. The last performance of a substantial work, Der Tod Jesu, took place in 1832. Interest revived in the 20th century and led to the Bärenreiter critical edition of the 1950s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Georg Philipp Telemann” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Oper am Gänsemarkt, opened in 1678, was the first public opera house in Germany, where Reinhard Keiser and the young George Frideric Handel worked; <b>Georg Philipp Telemann</b> was the city's director of music from 1721 to 1767, followed by Carl Philipp Emanuel Bach.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Johann Sebastian Bach was born in Eisenach in 1685, the son of a town musician, and <b>Georg Philipp Telemann</b> served as Kapellmeister at the Eisenach court.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-eisenach">Duchy of Saxe-Eisenach</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.13,11.64&amp;year=1736" aria-label="Magdeburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4812 363 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Georg Philipp Telemann"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5262 633h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5262 633h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.13,11.64&amp;year=1736">What was here in 1736? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
